In this way, E2 disguises their need for love as an illusion of abundance (false abundance); their sadness as joy; their lack of spontaneity as impulsivity; their repression as freedom; their lack of respect for others as excessive attention and submission; and their lack of commitment as empty promises.

The interpersonal strategy of the sexual E2 is, therefore, based on their own illusions about how to fulfill their need for love. It’s hidden in beliefs such as:

They have the right to obtain love through manipulation and seduction;

They are exceptional and deserve love immediately;

What they lacked in the past is owed to them now;

Earning admiration means receiving love;

Narcissistic self-sufficiency equates to real autonomy and freedom: they don’t actually need others that much;

They are capable of fulfilling others’ needs and becoming indispensable; and

They are always fully ready for relationships.

Regarding that last belief, in reality, they are often incapable of forming deep emotional bonds. They seem to long for exactly what terrifies them the most: a true loving relationship, which cannot be achieved without the ability to build connections based on stability and equality.

And yet, their core strategy, as we know, is seduction, driven by the neurotic need to generate desire in others. They are keenly aware that in a world where erotic desire is “sold” as a key to personal success, it’s a highly effective strategy for being wanted. Even when they recognize their own self-deception, they struggle to let go of the secondary benefit that comes from the magic of attraction. Over the years, they have perfected their skills, but they’ve forgotten that seduction was meant to be a means to an end, not the goal itself.

The irrational beliefs tied to this are: “If I’m not intensely desired, it means I’m not loved or that I’m worthless,” and “If someone doesn’t reciprocate everything I give, it means they don’t love me or that I’m worthless.”
